[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f39a2c38-13d6-5195-913b-3b870beaa4d7",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-11t,\\,y=5{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t-11,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=15{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t-11 & =0 \\\\ 6t & =11 \\\\ t & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{11}{6}) & =15{(\\frac{11}{6})}^{2}=\\frac{605}{12}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{11}{6}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "54766a60-bff0-5954-9adf-58afb3a8e68c",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "35a73cce-05e2-5435-bdd4-6479d58c961e",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{11}{6}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "35a73cce-05e2-5435-bdd4-6479d58c961e"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "generation": {
