    "question": {
      ":question/id": "83f5642f-980c-5610-a109-47cb5bd90798",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{6t},\\,y=5{t}^{2}-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{6t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10t-2\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n10t-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\frac{1}{5})=6{e}^{\\frac{6}{5}}=6 e^{\\frac{6}{5}}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{1}{5}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
