      ":question/id": "4920fc0f-ba9a-5829-86f9-91cdb5a66274",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-6t,\\,y=5{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t-6,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=15{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t-6 & =0 \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =15{(1)}^{2}=15\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=1.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "10bdec0d-f53b-5346-a47d-08a117ba9487",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
